# Response tiers — a query API's cheap answer must ROUTE, never SATISFY [SPEC:LTS-TIER]

> `lts_traverse` truncates against `token_budget`. Measured 2026-08-13 against [SPEC:CXC-VISION] at a budget of
> 2,500: **4 of 10 nodes, `truncated: true`, and NOT ONE of the five pillars** — a root, two cited document roots
> and a placement footnote, which is a coherent-looking vision containing none of what the vision is. The response
> is honest at the PROTOCOL level (`truncated`, `cursor` and `total` are all present) and dishonest at the
> ERGONOMIC level, because the easy path silently returns a fifth of the answer and callers do not read the flag.
> The rule that falls out is **truncation is a lie; an error is a fact**: a `digest` that is always COMPLETE for
> what it claims, a `page` whose window IS its claim, and a `full` that ERRORS at its ceiling rather than
> truncating. And the subtle part, which is the whole note and is David's correction to the obvious design: **a
> digest must be DELIBERATELY INSUFFICIENT.** The instinct to make it more useful with a summary or an excerpt is
> precisely backwards — a summary is a partial answer wearing a complete answer's clothes, the same failure as
> truncation packaged more attractively, and it trades an agent that KNOWS it is missing something for one that is
> CONFIDENT it is not. It must answer *is there something here I need to read?* and must NOT answer *what does it
> say?*. That is [CXC-VISION-COMPACT-WIRE] applied to a query API: the wire carries a fingerprint you cannot
> decode and must resolve at the registry; the digest carries an id and a size you cannot reason from and must
> resolve at the node. Both are compression that REFUSES TO BE SELF-SUFFICIENT BY DESIGN, and both carry the same
> corollary in the same load-bearing way — the thing you are forced to go to must be trivially reachable, or a
> size problem has merely been traded for an access problem.

- what-this-is: A DESIGN NOTE recording why a query API's cheap tier must ROUTE rather than SATISFY, and why that discipline is the compact-wire pillar at a second scale. It is not implementable and dispatches no builder: the layer it describes is already built.
- the-rule-in-one-line: Truncation is a lie; an error is a fact — and a digest rich enough to stop you reading is the same lie in better clothes.
- build-status: BUILT and LIVE. `factlattice_engine/tiers.py` implements the three tiers and `find` / `node` / `traverse` are all wired to them (commit d2f3d4c, 2026-08-13). This document is the design record catching up to a build, not a proposal preceding one.
- why-it-exists-as-a-corpus-node-at-all: The rationale currently lives ONLY in a Python module docstring, and the engine source cites `[LTS-TIER]` in ten places against a node id that resolved to NOTHING in any registered corpus until this document minted it. A design rule that code references and the graph cannot answer is unqueryable by every agent that reads through the corpus rather than the tree.
- relationship-to-the-vision: SIBLING, not a sixth pillar. [SPEC:CXC-VISION] claims FIVE recurring patterns and stakes its value on that recurrence being CHECKABLE; it is also re-hydrated into two agent bootstraps at every session start, so anything added under it is paid for at every session boot. This note is a NEW APPLICATION of one pillar and links to it by declared edge instead.
- evidence-base: ONE measurement, run live against the `intelligence-console` corpus on 2026-08-13 and reproduced at five budgets, plus one design ruling by David the same day. It is a single instance generalized into a rule, and the generalization rests on the argument at [CXC-TIER-TRUNCATION-IS-A-LIE], not on a population.
- blast-radius: The rule constrains every response shape the QUERY layer serves, and [DD-TIER-DEFAULT] would change what every existing caller of `lts_traverse` receives. Treat a change here as a change to the tool surface, not to a document.
- placement: UNRATIFIED. Written into `corpora/platform-specs/` because [LEC-QUERY] — the contract this extends — already lives here, and because `corpora/factlattice-parser/` is empty and deliberately UNPROFILED, so every profile-driven lint rule would be INERT there and a clean verdict would mean nothing. It is the FOURTH consecutive file in this directory flagged UNRATIFIED; see [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT].
- see-also: [CXC-VISION-COMPACT-WIRE], [SPEC:CXC-VISION], [SPEC:LTS-ENGINE-CONSOLIDATION], [LEC-QUERY], [CXC-VISION-RECOVERY], [CXC-VISION-PLACEMENT]

